== 2. Christ Much Exercised Himself in Secret Prayer

Secondly, consider that when Christ was on earth He exercised Himself much in secret prayer. He was often with God alone, as you#super[+] may see in these famous scriptures: “After He had sent the crowds away, He went up on the mountain by Himself to pray. When evening came, He was there alone” (Matt 14:23). Christ’s choosing solitary places for private prayer not only hints to us the danger of distraction and wandering of thoughts in prayer, but shows how necessary it is for us to choose the most suitable places we can for private prayers. Our own fickleness and Satan’s restlessness call on us to get into such corners where we may most freely pour out our souls into the bosom of God. “Early in the morning, a great while before day, Jesus got up and slipped out to a solitary place to pray” (Mark 1:35). As the morning is the fittest time for prayer, so solitary places are the fittest places for prayer. “After bidding them farewell, He went up on the mountain to pray” (Mark 6:46). He who would pray effectually needs to be quiet when he is alone. “Yet He frequently withdrew to the wilderness to pray” (Luke 5:16). The Greek has, He “was withdrawing and praying,” to let us understand that He did this often. When Christ was busy neither in teaching nor in working miracles, He was then very intent on private prayer. “In those days Jesus went out to the mountain to pray, and He spent all night in prayer to God” (Luke 6:12). Did Christ spend whole nights in private prayer to save our souls, and shall we think it too much to spend an hour or two in the day for the furtherance of the internal and eternal welfare of our souls?

“Every day Jesus taught at the temple, but every evening He went out to spend the night on the Mount of Olives” (Luke 21:37). Christ frequently joins praying and preaching together, and what Christ has joined together, let no man presume to separate. “Jesus went out as usual to the Mount of Olives, and the disciples followed Him.… And He withdrew about a stone’s throw beyond them, where He knelt down and prayed.… And in His anguish, He prayed more earnestly, and His sweat became like great drops of blood (clotted or congealed blood) falling to the ground (never was a garden watered with blood, before or since, as this was). When Jesus rose from prayer and returned to the disciples, He found them asleep, exhausted from sorrow” (Luke 22:39, 41, 44–45). Ah, what sad pieces of frailty the best of men are in an hour of trial and temptation! These very men, who a little before had boldly professed and promised that they would never leave Him nor forsake Him, and that they would go to prison for Christ and die for Christ, could not so much as watch with Him one hour when the day of trial came. They had neither eyes to see nor hands to wipe off Christ’s bloody sweat. So John 6:15–17. Thus you#super[+] see by all these famous instances that Christ was frequent in private prayer. Oh, that we would daily set this noble pattern before ourselves for our imitation, and make it our business, our work, our heaven, to copy this blessed pattern that Christ has set us: to be much with God alone! Certainly Christianity is nothing else but an imitation of the divine nature, a man’s bringing himself back to the image of God in which he was “created … in true righteousness and holiness” (Eph 4:24). A Christian’s whole life should be nothing but a visible representation of Christ. The heathen had this notion among them, as Lactantius reports, that the way to honor their gods was to be like them. I am sure that the highest way of honoring Christ is to be like Christ: “Whoever claims to abide in Him must walk as Jesus walked” (1 John 2:6). Oh, that this blessed Scripture might always lie warm on our hearts! Christ is the sun, and all the watches of our lives should be set by the dial of His motion. Christ is a pattern of patterns; His example should be to us instead of a thousand examples. It is not only our liberty, but our duty and glory, to follow Christ absolutely in all His moral virtues. Other patterns are imperfect and defective, but Christ is a perfect pattern; and of all His children, they are the happiest who come nearest to this perfect pattern.

Heliogabalus loved his children better for resembling him in sin. But Christ loves His children more for resembling Him in sanctity. I have read of some springs that change the color of the cattle that drink from them to the color of their own waters, as Du Bartas sings:

Cerona, Xanth, and Cephisus do make

The thirsty flocks, that of their waters take,

Black, red, and white; and near the crimson deep,

The Arabian fountain makes crimson sheep.

Certainly Jesus Christ is such a fountain: whoever bathes in it, and whoever drinks of it, will be changed into the same likeness (2 Cor 3:18).

#emph[Question]. But why was our Lord Jesus so much in private prayer? Why was He so often with God alone?

#emph[Answer 1]. First, it was to put a very high honor and value on private prayer; it was to enhance and raise the worth of this duty. Men are naturally very apt and prone to have low and undervaluing thoughts of secret prayer. But Christ, by exercising Himself in it so frequently, has put an everlasting honor and an inestimable value on it.

#emph[Answer 2]. Secondly, He was much in private prayer, He was often with God alone, so that He might not be seen by men and might avoid all show and appearance of ostentation and popular applause. He who has commanded us to “abstain from every appearance of evil” (1 Thess 5:22) would not Himself, when He was in this world, venture on the least appearance of evil. Christ was very wary of everything that so much as looked like sin; He was very wary of the very show and shadow of pride or vainglory.

#emph[Answer 3]. Thirdly, to avoid interruptions in the duty. Secrecy is no small help in carrying on a private duty seriously and with life. Interruptions and disturbances from outside are often water thrown on the coals of private prayer. Even the best Christians do no better than bungle when they meet with interruptions in their private devotions.

#emph[Answer 4]. Fourthly, to set us such a blessed pattern and gracious example that we should never be pleased or content with public prayers only, nor with family prayers only, but should also apply ourselves to secret prayer, to closet prayer. Christ was not always in public, nor always in His family, but He was often in private with God alone, so that by His own example He might encourage us to be often with God in secret. Happy are those who tread in His steps and who copy His pattern.

#emph[Answer 5]. Fifthly, that He might prove Himself, to our understandings and consciences, a most just and faithful High Priest (Heb 2:17; John 17). Christ was wonderfully faithful and careful in both parts of His priestly office: satisfaction and intercession. He was His people’s only spokesman. Ah, how earnest, how frequent He was in pouring out prayers, and tears, and sighs, and groans for His people in secret when He was in this world (Heb 5:7)! And now that He is in heaven, He is always making intercession for them (Heb 7:25).

#emph[Answer 6]. Sixthly, to convince us that His Father hears and observes our private prayers, and bottles up all our secret tears, and that He is no stranger to our closet desires, wrestlings, breathings, hungerings, and thirstings.
